/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Scale compares a length on the paper with the same length on the ground.
   - RF is the representative fraction, a pure number; map distance is the length measured on the sheet; ground distance is the horizontal length on the ground in the same unit; M is the denominator, the number of ground units that one map unit 
   - These are horizontal distances.
   - The two lengths are in the same unit, so the units cancel and an RF carries no unit at all.
   - Large scale means a large fraction. 1:1,000 is larger than 1:50,000, because 1/1000 is greater than 1/50,000.
   - A printed RF is useless to a reader holding only a divider.
   - That is a plain scale.
   - A diagonal scale reads finer.
   - A vernier scale reaches the same fineness with a short auxiliary scale drawn beside the main one.
   - A graphical, or comparative, scale is a bar drawn for two unit systems, kilometres below and miles above, so a reader switches units without arithmetic.
*/
