/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The geoid is the equipotential surface of earth's gravity field that best fits mean sea level, continued under the continents.
   - An ellipsoid is a smooth mathematical surface made by rotating an ellipse about its short axis.
   - A datum pairs an ellipsoid with a position and orientation on the earth.
   - Orthometric height H is the height of a point above the geoid, measured along the plumb line (the local vertical).
   - GNSS receivers give ellipsoidal height h, measured above the ellipsoid.
   - H is orthometric height in metres; h is ellipsoidal height in metres; N is geoid undulation in metres, positive when the geoid lies above the ellipsoid.
   - If N is negative, the geoid lies below the ellipsoid and H is larger than h.
   - Geographic coordinates give a point by latitude φ, longitude λ (angle east or west of the prime meridian, which is 0° longitude) and a height.
   - Projected coordinates flatten the ellipsoid onto a map plane using a map projection.
   - Cartesian coordinates use three perpendicular axes.
*/
